; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Circles from Small Steps
; CATEGORY: Shapes & Polygons
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     circle, polygon, pi, radius
; SUMMARY:  Shows how a polygon with more and more sides turns into a circle, then draws circles of any radius.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The top row shows shapes with 5, 8, 16 and 72 sides, all the
;   same size. Watch them get rounder. The bottom row is a target
;   made of circles drawn with a CIRCLE procedure that takes a
;   radius, just like a compass.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Then try  circle 100  in the command center.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * a circle is "walk a tiny bit, turn a tiny bit", many times
;   * the distance round a circle is 2 x pi x radius
;   * dividing that distance into 36 steps gives the step length
;   * how to start a circle from its center instead of its edge
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change 72 in the top row to 360. Can you see any difference?
;   * Use 12 steps in CIRCLE instead of 36. Is it still round enough?
;   * Draw the Olympic rings with five calls to CIRCLEAT.
; ============================================================

; A polygon that fits round a circle of the given radius.
; Each side is the circumference shared out between the sides.
to roundpoly :sides :radius
  localmake "side 2 * pi * :radius / :sides
  repeat :sides [forward :side right 360 / :sides]
end

; 36 steps of 10 degrees each. Our eyes cannot tell this from
; a perfect circle unless it is very big.
to circle :radius
  roundpoly 36 :radius
end

; The turtle draws a circle starting at the edge, so to put the
; CENTER at (x, y) we start at the leftmost point facing up.
to circleat :x :y :radius
  penup setxy :x - :radius :y setheading 0 pendown
  circle :radius
end

; same idea for the top row, so all four shapes line up
to polyat :x :y :sides :radius
  penup setxy :x - :radius :y setheading 0 pendown
  roundpoly :sides :radius
end

; A target: circles that share a center, each one bigger.
to target :x :y
  for [i 1 6] [
    setpencolor item :i [4 14 6 2 1 13]
    circleat :x :y 15 * :i
  ]
end

clearscreen
setpensize 3

; the top row: 5, 8, 16 and 72 sides
setpencolor 1
foreach [5 8 16 72] [
  polyat -300 + 200 * (# - 1) 150 ? 60
  penup setxy -310 + 200 * (# - 1) 60 setpencolor 0
  label (sentence ? "sides)
  setpencolor 1
]

; the bottom row: a target and two single circles
target -150 -140
setpencolor 10 circleat 100 -140 40
setpencolor 5 circleat 250 -140 80
hideturtle
